      "updated": 1783140646838,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Notes\n\nUploading a file\n1. Poor Approach - Store file in Server's local storage i.e. on File Service local storage and store the actual metadata in DynamoDB/Postgres\n    - Hit storage problem eventually\n    - Need to un-necessary horizontal scale\n    - Unreliable as what if server goes down.\n2. Workable Approach - User uploads file to File Service, it then uploads to Blob Storage(S3), then we update FileMetadata in DynamoDB along with S3 link\n    - Need to handle case where metadata saved but file not uploaded.\n    - or File uploaded but metadata not saved.\n    - Biggest pain - Upload twice user -> File Service -> S3\n3. Best solution - Allow user to directly upload to S3 through pre-signed URL\n    - Once file successfully uploaded by client, S3 sends notification to File Service to update the saved metadata\n    - pre-signed url contains session tokens in the URL itself, valid for small time and doesn't require validation, anyone with the url can upload\n    - now the process is : \n        1. Request par from FileService(generates using S3 SDK) and save file metadata in DynamoDB with status \"uploading\"\n            This updates our API\n            POST /files/presigned-url -> PresignedUrl\n            Request:\n            {\n              FileMetadata\n            }\n        2. Use par to upload directly to S3 from client via PUT request where the file is body of request\n        3. Once uploaded, S3 sends notification to File Service and then it updates the metadata to \"uploaded\"\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Notes\n\nUploading a file\n1. Poor Approach - Store file in Server's local storage i.e. on File Service local storage and store the actual metadata in DynamoDB/Postgres\n    - Hit storage problem eventually\n    - Need to un-necessary horizontal scale\n    - Unreliable as what if server goes down.\n2. Workable Approach - User uploads file to File Service, it then uploads to Blob Storage(S3), then we update FileMetadata in DynamoDB along with S3 link\n    - Need to handle case where metadata saved but file not uploaded.\n    - or File uploaded but metadata not saved.\n    - Biggest pain - Upload twice user -> File Service -> S3\n3. Best solution - Allow user to directly upload to S3 through pre-signed URL\n    - Once file successfully uploaded by client, S3 sends notification to File Service to update the saved metadata\n    - pre-signed url contains session tokens in the URL itself, valid for small time and doesn't require validation, anyone with the url can upload\n    - now the process is : \n        1. Request par from FileService(generates using S3 SDK) and save file metadata in DynamoDB with status \"uploading\"\n            This updates our API\n            POST /files/presigned-url -> PresignedUrl\n            Request:\n            {\n              FileMetadata\n            }\n        2. Use par to upload directly to S3 from client via PUT request where the file is body of request\n        3. Once uploaded, S3 sends notification to File Service and then it updates the metadata to \"uploaded\"\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "TLwoucY4F619tSRN2Tqhf",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -11086.80659813315,
      "y": 33775.185052299355,
      "width": 1272.3192138671875,
      "height": 350,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a6",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 760731602,
      "version": 818,
      "versionNonce": 875644942,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1783143051303,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Downloading the file\n1. Poor Approach - through File Service\n    - Blob Storage to File Service and then to client\n    - bad as we download the file twice\n    - slow\n2. Good Solution - Download directly from Blob Storage\n    - user par to download directly from S3\n    - This changes our API of get file\n        GET /files/{fileId}/presigned-url -> PresignedUrl\n    - The only limitation is this can still be slow for large files as the S3 could be located far from user\n3. Great Solution - Download from CDN\n    - cache the file closer to user\n    - use similar par to download from CDN\n    - The only challenge is CDN is expensive, i.e. to layout a network of cache servers across the globe and then maintaining them.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Downloading the file\n1. Poor Approach - through File Service\n    - Blob Storage to File Service and then to client\n    - bad as we download the file twice\n    - slow\n2. Good Solution - Download directly from Blob Storage\n    - user par to download directly from S3\n    - This changes our API of get file\n        GET /files/{fileId}/presigned-url -> PresignedUrl\n    - The only limitation is this can still be slow for large files as the S3 could be located far from user\n3. Great Solution - Download from CDN\n    - cache the file closer to user\n    - use similar par to download from CDN\n    - The only challenge is CDN is expensive, i.e. to layout a network of cache servers across the globe and then maintaining them.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "zmV5Bz3LrG6RGFJK3aQuh",
