      "updated": 1783147207275,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Auto syncing the file\n\nLocal -> Remote\n    - User updates a file on their local machine, this needs to be synced to remote server\n    - Remote storage is source of truth for all users so this needs to be consistent asap\n    - Monitors the local Dropbox folder for changes using OS-specific file system events\n        - FileSystemWatcher of Windows\n        - FSEvents on macOS\n    - When change detected, queue the modified file for upload locally\n    - Uses the same upload API to update file and metadata\n    - Conflicts are resolved using \"last write wins\" strategy i.e. two users editing the same file most recent edit is saved\n    - update versioning if in scope\n\nRemote -> Local\n    - each client needs to know when changes happen to remote server so they can pull latest version of that file\n    - 2 Approaches\n        - Polling : client periodically asks \"has anything changes since my last sync?\"\n          server queries DB to see if any file this user is watching has a updatedAt TS newer than last sync\n          Slow to detect changes, wastes bandwidth if nothing has changes.\n        - WebSocket of SSE : server maintains an open connection with each client and push notification when changes occur.\n            More complex but provides real-time updates\n    - Use Hybrid approach\n        - Each client maintains a single WebSocket(or SSE) to the server one per device/session\n        - Active Notification : server pushes changes as they happen\n        - Periodic polling as a safety net : WebSockets connection can drop, messages can be missed\n            to overcome this client periodically(every few mins) polls server for  changes using our sync api\n            This ensures eventual consistency if WebSocket connection was temporarily interrupted.\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Auto syncing the file\n\nLocal -> Remote\n    - User updates a file on their local machine, this needs to be synced to remote server\n    - Remote storage is source of truth for all users so this needs to be consistent asap\n    - Monitors the local Dropbox folder for changes using OS-specific file system events\n        - FileSystemWatcher of Windows\n        - FSEvents on macOS\n    - When change detected, queue the modified file for upload locally\n    - Uses the same upload API to update file and metadata\n    - Conflicts are resolved using \"last write wins\" strategy i.e. two users editing the same file most recent edit is saved\n    - update versioning if in scope\n\nRemote -> Local\n    - each client needs to know when changes happen to remote server so they can pull latest version of that file\n    - 2 Approaches\n        - Polling : client periodically asks \"has anything changes since my last sync?\"\n          server queries DB to see if any file this user is watching has a updatedAt TS newer than last sync\n          Slow to detect changes, wastes bandwidth if nothing has changes.\n        - WebSocket of SSE : server maintains an open connection with each client and push notification when changes occur.\n            More complex but provides real-time updates\n    - Use Hybrid approach\n        - Each client maintains a single WebSocket(or SSE) to the server one per device/session\n        - Active Notification : server pushes changes as they happen\n        - Periodic polling as a safety net : WebSockets connection can drop, messages can be missed\n            to overcome this client periodically(every few mins) polls server for  changes using our sync api\n            This ensures eventual consistency if WebSocket connection was temporarily interrupted.\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
