    {
      "id": "4HJLQUajRemf5ecD8HEpO",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -8696.646008141874,
      "y": 32623.220670532093,
      "width": 1415.55908203125,
      "height": 1600,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "au",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1161651666,
      "version": 2676,
      "versionNonce": 1607586766,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1783150857713,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "How to support large files? (50 GB)\n- For user experience it should have progress indicator and Resumable Uploads so that if they loose internet they don't have to restart\n- Limitations to uploading a large file via single POST request\n    - Timeouts : web servers have timeout setting to prevent infinite waiting for a 50GB file it could easily timeout\n        - 50BG file with 100Mbps internet speed -> 50GB * 8bits/byte / 100Mbps = 4000s = 1.11Hour\n    - Browser and Server limitation : Size of the payload does not support generally more than 10MB \n    - Network Interruptions : Internet connection drops\n    - User Experience : users are blind to the progress\n\n- To overcome all this we use \"chunking\" i.e. breaking file into smaller pieces and uploading them one at a time(or in parallel if bandwidth available)\n- chunking needs to be done on client as that's where we're uploading from\n- 5-10 MB chunks depending on network conditions and file size.\n- Now easy to show progress bar based on success upload of each chunk\n\n\nHow to handle resumable uploads?\n- Need to keep track of what chunks are uploaded and which aren't\n- Saving state of upload in FileMetadata table, schema looks like below:\n    {\n      \"id\": \"123\",\n      \"name\": \"file.txt\",\n      \"size\": 1000,\n      \"mimeType\": \"text/plain\",\n      \"uploadedBy\": \"user1\",\n      \"status\": \"uploading\",\n      \"chunks\": [\n        {\n          \"id\": \"chunk1\",\n          \"status\": \"uploaded\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"chunk2\",\n          \"status\": \"uploading\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"chunk3\",\n          \"status\": \"not-uploaded\"\n        }\n      ]\n    }\n- when user resumes upload, we check \"chunks\" field to see what's upload and what's not\n\n- How to keep chunks field in sync with actual chunks that's been uploaded?\n    1. Good Solution : Update based on client PATCH request\n        - client takes file -> chunks it -> uploads chunks directly to S3\n        - S3 responds to each chunk upload with success message\n        - upon receiving on client by S3, client sends a PATCH request to File Service to update \"chunks\" field in FileMetadata table\n            PATCH /files/{fileId}/chunks\n            Request:\n            {\n              \"chunks\": [\n                {\n                  \"id\": \"chunk1\",\n                  \"status\": \"uploaded\"\n                },\n              ]\n            }\n        - Issue is we're trusting client to keep chunks field in sync this could be a security risk\n        - A malicious user could send PATCH to mark all chunks uploaded without actually uploading them\n        \n      2. Server-Side Chunk Verification\n        - Same as above, but each chunk gets an ETag upon success upload, client includes it in PATCH request\n        - BE verifies these ETags by calling S3's ListParts API, providing an efficient way to validate multiple chunks at once\n        - In short, we trust but verify!",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "How to support large files? (50 GB)\n- For user experience it should have progress indicator and Resumable Uploads so that if they loose internet they don't have to restart\n- Limitations to uploading a large file via single POST request\n    - Timeouts : web servers have timeout setting to prevent infinite waiting for a 50GB file it could easily timeout\n        - 50BG file with 100Mbps internet speed -> 50GB * 8bits/byte / 100Mbps = 4000s = 1.11Hour\n    - Browser and Server limitation : Size of the payload does not support generally more than 10MB \n    - Network Interruptions : Internet connection drops\n    - User Experience : users are blind to the progress\n\n- To overcome all this we use \"chunking\" i.e. breaking file into smaller pieces and uploading them one at a time(or in parallel if bandwidth available)\n- chunking needs to be done on client as that's where we're uploading from\n- 5-10 MB chunks depending on network conditions and file size.\n- Now easy to show progress bar based on success upload of each chunk\n\n\nHow to handle resumable uploads?\n- Need to keep track of what chunks are uploaded and which aren't\n- Saving state of upload in FileMetadata table, schema looks like below:\n    {\n      \"id\": \"123\",\n      \"name\": \"file.txt\",\n      \"size\": 1000,\n      \"mimeType\": \"text/plain\",\n      \"uploadedBy\": \"user1\",\n      \"status\": \"uploading\",\n      \"chunks\": [\n        {\n          \"id\": \"chunk1\",\n          \"status\": \"uploaded\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"chunk2\",\n          \"status\": \"uploading\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"id\": \"chunk3\",\n          \"status\": \"not-uploaded\"\n        }\n      ]\n    }\n- when user resumes upload, we check \"chunks\" field to see what's upload and what's not\n\n- How to keep chunks field in sync with actual chunks that's been uploaded?\n    1. Good Solution : Update based on client PATCH request\n        - client takes file -> chunks it -> uploads chunks directly to S3\n        - S3 responds to each chunk upload with success message\n        - upon receiving on client by S3, client sends a PATCH request to File Service to update \"chunks\" field in FileMetadata table\n            PATCH /files/{fileId}/chunks\n            Request:\n            {\n              \"chunks\": [\n                {\n                  \"id\": \"chunk1\",\n                  \"status\": \"uploaded\"\n                },\n              ]\n            }\n        - Issue is we're trusting client to keep chunks field in sync this could be a security risk\n        - A malicious user could send PATCH to mark all chunks uploaded without actually uploading them\n        \n      2. Server-Side Chunk Verification\n        - Same as above, but each chunk gets an ETag upon success upload, client includes it in PATCH request\n        - BE verifies these ETags by calling S3's ListParts API, providing an efficient way to validate multiple chunks at once\n        - In short, we trust but verify!",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "XKWpNQjVhUFkF1xTqhCWP",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -8643.776157463955,
      "y": 34270.98215284139,
      "width": 1990.4786376953125,
      "height": 475,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "av",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 256736910,
      "version": 885,
      "versionNonce": 252825038,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1783152167065,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "How to uniquely identify a file and chunk?\n- Upon resuming 1. Have I tried to upload this file before? 2. If Yes, what chunks have I already uplaoded?\n- A fingerprint is a mathematical calculation to generate a unique hash based on contents of file\n   Created using SHA256, Using this fingerprint we can whether the file has been uploaded before (deduplication) and whether an in-progress upload can be resumed. \n- Note : two diff users uploading the same file have same fingerprint, fileId should be the unique identifier\n\n\n- To sum it all together\n    1. client chunks 50GB into 5-10MB and calculate fingerprint for each chunk, also fingerprint for entire file calculated to check for duplicates and resumability\n    2. The client will send a request to check if a file with the same fingerprint already exists for this user. \n        If it does and has a status of \"uploading\", the client can resume the upload by fetching the existing chunk statuses.\n    3. If the file does not exist, the client will POST a request to initiate a multipart upload. The backend will call S3's CreateMultipartUpload API to get an uploadId, \n       generate presigned URLs for each part, save the file metadata in the FileMetadata table with a status of \"uploading\", and return the uploadId along with presigned URLs for each chunk.\n    4. The client will then upload each chunk to S3 using its corresponding presigned URL (each part requires its own presigned URL with the uploadId and partNumber). After each chunk is uploaded,\n        the client sends a PATCH request to our backend with the chunk status and ETag. Our backend can then verify the chunk uploads with S3's ListParts API before updating the chunks field in the \n        FileMetadata table to mark the chunk as \"uploaded\".\n    5. Once all chunks in our chunks array are marked as \"uploaded\", the backend calls S3's CompleteMultipartUpload API with the list of part numbers and ETags. This tells S3 to assemble all the parts\n        into a single object. Only after S3 confirms successful assembly does the backend update the FileMetadata table to mark the file as \"uploaded\".\n- This is S3 Multipart Upload API",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "How to uniquely identify a file and chunk?\n- Upon resuming 1. Have I tried to upload this file before? 2. If Yes, what chunks have I already uplaoded?\n- A fingerprint is a mathematical calculation to generate a unique hash based on contents of file\n   Created using SHA256, Using this fingerprint we can whether the file has been uploaded before (deduplication) and whether an in-progress upload can be resumed. \n- Note : two diff users uploading the same file have same fingerprint, fileId should be the unique identifier\n\n\n- To sum it all together\n    1. client chunks 50GB into 5-10MB and calculate fingerprint for each chunk, also fingerprint for entire file calculated to check for duplicates and resumability\n    2. The client will send a request to check if a file with the same fingerprint already exists for this user. \n        If it does and has a status of \"uploading\", the client can resume the upload by fetching the existing chunk statuses.\n    3. If the file does not exist, the client will POST a request to initiate a multipart upload. The backend will call S3's CreateMultipartUpload API to get an uploadId, \n       generate presigned URLs for each part, save the file metadata in the FileMetadata table with a status of \"uploading\", and return the uploadId along with presigned URLs for each chunk.\n    4. The client will then upload each chunk to S3 using its corresponding presigned URL (each part requires its own presigned URL with the uploadId and partNumber). After each chunk is uploaded,\n        the client sends a PATCH request to our backend with the chunk status and ETag. Our backend can then verify the chunk uploads with S3's ListParts API before updating the chunks field in the \n        FileMetadata table to mark the chunk as \"uploaded\".\n    5. Once all chunks in our chunks array are marked as \"uploaded\", the backend calls S3's CompleteMultipartUpload API with the list of part numbers and ETags. This tells S3 to assemble all the parts\n        into a single object. Only after S3 confirms successful assembly does the backend update the FileMetadata table to mark the file as \"uploaded\".\n- This is S3 Multipart Upload API",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "3hGTVNNZnVeK3la765WXp",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -8630.253666523453,
      "y": 34839.21717826132,
      "width": 1840.9588623046875,
      "height": 75,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aw",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1475489486,
      "version": 44,
      "versionNonce": 1106153742,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1783152169374,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Downloading\nFor very large files, S3 and HTTP natively support Range requests, which let the client download different byte ranges in parallel or resume an interrupted download without starting over. \nThe client doesn't need to know anything about the original chunk boundaries.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Downloading\nFor very large files, S3 and HTTP natively support Range requests, which let the client download different byte ranges in parallel or resume an interrupted download without starting over. \nThe client doesn't need to know anything about the original chunk boundaries.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "1GNWZKUDVBj2-_G8C1f-n",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -8620.05967417895,
      "y": 34997.265014281504,
      "width": 1705.458740234375,
      "height": 450,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "ax",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1762411470,
      "version": 1939,
      "versionNonce": 1922141266,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1783153155286,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Making Syncs faster\n- Chunking helps here\n- When file changes, only sync the chunks that actually changes rather than the entire file\n- If used fixed size chunks (say 5MB), inserting a single byte near the beginning of file shifts all subsequent chunk boundaries causing every chunk afte the edit\n  to produce a different fingerprint, making delta syncs nearly useless.\n- Solution is use Content-Defined Chunking(CDC) where chunk boundaries are defined by file's content using a rolling hash(like Rabin fingerprinting)\n- With CDC, a small edit affects only the chunks immediately surrounding the change and vast majority of chunks remain identical.\n- This is what used in production.\n\nMaking uploads/downloads faster\n- Compress the chunks allowing to send fewer bytes over network\n- Happens on the client side and S3 stores the compressed version as is, and when downloaded the decompression happens at client\n- Only useful when speed gained from transferring fewer bytes outweighs time taken to compress and decompress.\n- Media files like audio and video are already byte data very low compression ratio do not compress where as a text file very good compression ratio 5GB -> 1GB do compress\n- Implement a logic on client to decide all this based on file type, size and network conditions.\n- Gzip(most widely accepted), Brotli(better than Gzip especially for txt), Zstandard(most effective, can be tuned)\n- TIP : Always compress before you encrypt, because encryption naturally introduces randomness to file, making it difficult to compress.\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Making Syncs faster\n- Chunking helps here\n- When file changes, only sync the chunks that actually changes rather than the entire file\n- If used fixed size chunks (say 5MB), inserting a single byte near the beginning of file shifts all subsequent chunk boundaries causing every chunk afte the edit\n  to produce a different fingerprint, making delta syncs nearly useless.\n- Solution is use Content-Defined Chunking(CDC) where chunk boundaries are defined by file's content using a rolling hash(like Rabin fingerprinting)\n- With CDC, a small edit affects only the chunks immediately surrounding the change and vast majority of chunks remain identical.\n- This is what used in production.\n\nMaking uploads/downloads faster\n- Compress the chunks allowing to send fewer bytes over network\n- Happens on the client side and S3 stores the compressed version as is, and when downloaded the decompression happens at client\n- Only useful when speed gained from transferring fewer bytes outweighs time taken to compress and decompress.\n- Media files like audio and video are already byte data very low compression ratio do not compress where as a text file very good compression ratio 5GB -> 1GB do compress\n- Implement a logic on client to decide all this based on file type, size and network conditions.\n- Gzip(most widely accepted), Brotli(better than Gzip especially for txt), Zstandard(most effective, can be tuned)\n- TIP : Always compress before you encrypt, because encryption naturally introduces randomness to file, making it difficult to compress.\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
